In Axios 1.8.2, while looking into CVE-2025-27152 and the GHSA-jr5f-v2jv-69x6 PoC, I found that an absolute URL can still override the configured `baseURL` unless `allowAbsoluteUrls` is explicitly set to `false`. The original test case is:

```javascript
// import axios from "axios";

const axios = require('axios')

// The expectation is to send a request to port 12313.
const internalAPIClient = axios.create({
  baseURL: "http://127.0.0.1:12313/api/v1/users/",
  headers: {
    "X-API-KEY": "1234567890",
  },
});

// const userId = "123";
// The attacker passes in //127.0.0.1:12314/
const userId = "http://127.0.0.1:12314/";

internalAPIClient.get(userId);
```

with `package.json`:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "axios": "^1.8.2"
  }
}
```

A commenter noted that in the fix, if `allowAbsoluteUrls` is undefined then `allowAbsoluteUrls == false` is false, so the `baseURL` and requested URL are not combined:

```javascript
let allowAbsoluteUrls // undefined
console.log(allowAbsoluteUrls == false) // false
```

The maintainer said this is an explicit opt-in change for the 1.x line. However, it seems the `allowAbsoluteUrls` parameter is not part of `AxiosRequestConfig`. Does anyone know how we can set it to `false` to avoid this vulnerability? It appears to be used internally in `axios/lib/adapters/http.js` to call `buildFullPath` in `axios/lib/core/buildFullPath.js`, and modifying its value is complex due to how it is integrated into the configuration. I tried setting it to `false`, but I am having issues in test cases and mocking is challenging; the error is:

```text
TypeError: URL is not a constructor


  > 1 | import axios, {
      | ^  
    2 | AxiosProgressEvent,
```

